id, {sidecars={proxy_pid}})\n      and os.exit()s with the returned code. parent maps wait status\n      to an exit code and returns it from run().\n\n Setup failures are reported over a CLOEXEC error pipe (the same\n pattern proxy.start uses for its bound port): the supervisor and the\n pre-exec workload write the failing step there before exiting, and a\n successful execve closes the workload's end automatically. run()\n drains the pipe after wait() — a non-empty pipe always means setup\n failed before the workload ran, so run() returns nil + the message\n instead of masquerading the failure as a workload exit code.", records={{fields={{"capabilities", "function(): types.Capabilities", ""}}, line=53, name="CapsModule"}, {fields={{"run", "function(opts: BoxOptions, argv: {string}): integer | nil, string", ""}}, line=320, name="BoxRunModule"}}}, ["cosmic.quicksand.caps"]={examples={}, file="lib/cosmic/quicksand/caps.tl", functions={{description=" Resolve a capability name (e.g. \"CAP_NET_RAW\") to its integer number\n for the running host, or nil + error if the name is unknown or the\n host does not define it.", is_local=true, line=54, name="number_of", params={{description="capability name", name="name", param_type="string"}}, returns={{description="capability number", return_type="integer?"}, {description="error message", return_type="string?"}}, signature="(name: string): integer | nil, string"}, {description=" True when the capability bounding-set API is wired up on this host\n (Linux with prctl + the CAP_* / PR_CAPBSET_* constants). A true\n result means the calls exist; actually dropping from the bounding\n set additionally requires CAP_SETPCAP at runtime.", is_local=true, line=71, name="supported", params={}, returns={{description="supported", return_type="boolean"}}, signature="(): boolean"}, {description=" Build a capset(2) bitmask from capability names: bit N (1 << number)\n is set for each named capability. Suitable for the effective /\n permitted / inheritable arguments of unix.capset.", is_local=true, line=85, name="mask", params={{description="capability names", name="names", param_type="{string}"}}, returns={{description="bitmask", return_type="integer?"}, {description="error message if a name is unknown", return_type="string?"}}, signature="(names: {string}): integer | nil, string"}, {description=" Read whether a capability is present in the current bounding set via\n PR_CAPBSET_READ.", is_local=true, line=101, name="bounding_read", params={{description="capability name", name="cap", param_type="string"}}, returns={{description="true if in the bounding set, false if not", return_type="boolean?"}, {description="error message on failure", return_type="string?"}}, signature="(cap: string): boolean | nil, string"}, {description=" Remove a single capability from the current bounding set via\n PR_CAPBSET_DROP. Irreversible and inherited across execve. Requires\n CAP_SETPCAP.", is_local=true, line=118, name="bounding_drop", params={{description="capability name", name="cap", param_type="string"}}, returns={{description="true on success", return_type="boolean"}, {description="error message on failure", return_type="string?"}}, signature="(cap: string): boolean, string"}, {description=" Drop every capability from the bounding set, optionally retaining\n the names in `opts.keep`. Iterates 0..CAP_LAST_CAP calling\n PR_CAPBSET_DROP. Irreversible and inherited across execve, so a\n workload can never acquire a dropped capability afterwards — not\n even by executing a setuid / file-capability binary.\n Must run while the caller still holds CAP_SETPCAP (e.g. immediately\n after unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER) with a uid map, before proc.drop_privs\n clears the capability set). A cap number the kernel does not know\n (EINVAL) is skipped; ENOSYS ends the sweep successfully. On a host\n without the CAP_* constants (non-Linux) this is a successful no-op.", is_local=true, line=143, name="drop_bounding", params={{description="optional keep-set", name="opts", param_type="DropOptions?"}}, returns={{description="true on success", return_type="boolean"}, {description="error message on failure", return_type="string?"}}, signature="(opts?: DropOptions): boolean, string"}}, internal=true, module_doc=" Linux capability bounding-set control for box assembly.\n\n Typed wrapper over cosmo.unix prctl(2) capability operations. The\n companion to cosmic.quicksand.proc: where proc.drop_privs clears the\n *current* capability set (effective/permitted/inheritable via\n capset), this module manipulates the capability *bounding set* — the\n ceiling that limits which capabilities a process (or anything it\n execve's) can ever hold. Dropping a capability from the bounding set\n is irreversible and inherited across fork/exec, so a workload can\n never regain it, even by executing a setuid or file-capability\n binary.\n\n Intended for the \"after fork, before exec\" window of a boxed child,\n while it still holds CAP_SETPCAP in its user namespace. Call\n drop_bounding() before proc.drop_privs (which clears CAP_SETPCAP and\n would make subsequent PR_CAPBSET_DROP calls fail with EPERM).\n\n Linux-only. On non-Linux hosts (or a cosmos build without the CAP_*\n constants) the constants are nil; drop_bounding() is then a\n successful no-op and supported() returns false.", records={{description=" Options for drop_bounding().", fields={{"keep", "{string}", ""}}, line=43, name="DropOptions"}, {fields={{"NAMES", "{string}", ""}, {"supported", "function(): boolean", ""}, {"number_of", "function(name: string): integer | nil, string", ""}, {"mask", "function(names: {string}): integer | nil, string", ""}, {"bounding_read", "function(cap: string): boolean | nil, string", ""}, {"bounding_drop", "function(cap: string): boolean, string", ""}, {"drop_bounding", "function(opts?: DropOptions): boolean, string", ""}}, line=177, name="CapsModule"}}}, ["cosmic.quicksand.netns"]={examples={{body="\n  local netns = require(\"cosmic.quicksand.netns\")\n  local fd, err = netns.open(nil)\n  if not fd then\n    io.write(\"no netns\\n\")\n    return\n  end\n  io.write(\"opened\\n\")\n  netns.close(fd)\n", description=" Example_open_self opens the current process's own netns file\n descriptor. Useful as a sanity check and as a reference for the\n fd-based enter() flow. On non-Linux hosts open() returns nil+err,\n which the example also handles.", line=7, name="Example_open_self"}, {body="\n  local spawn = require(\"cosmic.child\")\n  local cosmic = arg[-1]\n  local script = [[\nlocal ns = require(\"cosmic.quicksand.netns\")\nif not ns.unshare_user() then return end\nassert(ns.unshare())\nassert(ns.bring_up(\"lo\"))\n]]\n  local h = spawn.spawn({cosmic, \"-e\", script})\n  local hh = h as spawn.Handle\n  hh:wait()\n", description=" Example_unshare_in_subprocess creates a fresh network namespace\n and brings loopback up. unshare() is irreversible for the current\n thread, so this runs in a subprocess. No output check is attached\n because the result depends on whether the host allows\n unprivileged user-namespace creation.", line=23, name="Example_unshare_in_subprocess"}}, file="lib/cosmic/quicksand/netns.tl", functions={{description=" Build a `struct ifreq` for SIOCGIFFLAGS/SIOCSIFFLAGS: char\n ifr_name[IFNAMSIZ] at offset 0, int16 ifr_flags at offset IFNAMSIZ,\n padded to the largest union member.", is_local=true, line=29, name="build_ifreq", params={}, returns={}, signature="(name: string, flags: integer): string"}, {description=" Parse the 16-bit flags field out of an ifreq returned by ioctl().", is_local=true, line=40, name="parse_ifreq_flags", params={}, returns={}, signature="(ifr: string): integer"}, {description=" Open an AF_INET dgram socket for the SIOC* interface ioctls.\n SOCK_CLOEXEC: don't leak this short-lived control socket into any\n exec'd child.", is_local=true, line=47, name="control_socket", params={}, returns={}, signature="(): integer, string"}, {description=" Read the current IFF_* flags on `