 prctl, ptrace, rename,\n rmdir."}, {"ECANCELED", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ECHILD", "number", " @type integer No child process.\n Raised by `wait`, `waitpid`, `waitid`, `wait3`, `wait4`."}, {"ECONNABORTED", "number", " @type integer Connection reset before accept. Raised by `accept`."}, {"ECONNREFUSED", "number", " @type integer System-imposed limit on the number of threads was encountered.\n Raised by connect, listen, recv."}, {"ECONNRESET", "number", ""}, {"EDEADLK", "number", " @type integer Resource deadlock avoided.\n Raised by `fcntl`."}, {"EDESTADDRREQ", "number", " @type integer Destination address required. Raised by `send`, `write`."}, {"EDOM", "number", " @type integer"}, {"EDQUOT", "number", " @type integer Disk quota exceeded.\n Raised by link, mkdir, mknod, open, rename, symlink,\n write."}, {"EEXIST", "number", " @type integer File exists.\n Raised by `link`, `mkdir`, `mknod`, `mmap`, `open`, `rename`,\n `rmdir`, `symlink`."}, {"EFAULT", "number", " @type integer"}, {"EFBIG", "number", " @type integer File too large.\n Raised by `copy_file_range`, `open`, `truncate`, `write`."}, {"EFTYPE", "number", " @type integer Inappropriate file type or format."}, {"EHOSTDOWN", "number", " @type integer Host is down. Raised by `accept`."}, {"EHOSTUNREACH", "number", " @type integer Host is unreachable. Raised by `accept`."}, {"EHWPOISON", "number", " @type integer Memory page has hardware error."}, {"EIDRM", "number", " @type integer Identifier removed. Raised by `msgctl`."}, {"EILSEQ", "number", " @type integer"}, {"EINPROGRESS", "number", " @type integer"}, {"EINTR", "number", " @type integer The greatest of all errnos; crucial for building real time reliable software.\n Raised by `accept`, `clock_nanosleep`, `close`, `connect`, `dup`, `fcntl`,\n `flock`, `getrandom`, `nanosleep`, `open`, `pause`, `poll`, `ptrace`, `read`, `recv`,\n `select`, `send`, `sigsuspend`, `sigwaitinfo`, `truncate`, `wait`, `write`."}, {"EINVAL", "number", " @type integer Invalid argument.\n Raised by [pretty much everything]."}, {"EIO", "number", " @type integer\n Raised by `access`, `acct`, `chdir`, `chmod`, `chown`, `chroot`, `close`,\n `copy_file_range`, `execve`, `fallocate`, `fsync`, `ioperm`, `link`, `madvise`,\n `mbind`, `pciconfig_read`, `ptrace`, `read`, `readlink`, `sendfile`, `statfs`,\n `symlink`, `sync_file_range`, `truncate`, `unlink`, `write`."}, {"EISCONN", "number", ""}, {"EISDIR", "number", " @type integer Is a directory.\n Raised by `copy_file_range`, `execve`, `open`, `read`, `rename`, `truncate`,\n `unlink`."}, {"ELOOP", "number", " @type integer Too many levels of symbolic links.\n Raised by access, bind, chdir, chmod, chown, chroot, execve, link,\n mkdir, mknod, open, readlink, rename, rmdir, stat, symlink,\n truncate, unlink, utimensat."}, {"EMEDIUMTYPE", "number", " @type integer Wrong medium type. Raised by `mount`."}, {"EMFILE", "number", " @type integer Too many open files.\n Raised by `accept`, `dup`, `execve`, `fanotify_init`, `fcntl`,\n `open`, `pipe`, `socket`, `socketpair`, `timerfd_create`."}, {"EMLINK", "number", " @type integer Too many links;\n Raised by `link`, `mkdir`, `rename`."}, {"EMSGSIZE", "number", " @type integer Message too long. Raised by `send`."}, {"EMULTIHOP", "number", " @type integer Multihop attempted."}, {"ENAMETOOLONG", "number", " @type integer Filename too long. Cosmopolitan Libc currently defines `PATH_MAX` as\n 1024 characters. On UNIX that limit should only apply to system call\n wrappers like realpath. On Windows NT it's observed by all system\n calls that accept a pathname.\n Raised by `access`, `bind`, `chdir`, `chmod`, `chown`, `chroot`,\n `execve`, `gethostname`, `link`, `mkdir`, `mknod`, `open`,\n `readlink`, `rename`, `rmdir`, `stat`, `symlink`, `truncate`,\n `unlink`, `utimensat`."}, {"ENETDOWN", "number", " @type integer Network is down. Raised by `accept`."}, {"ENETRESET", "number", " @type integer Connection reset by network."}, {"ENETUNREACH", "number", " @type integer Host is unreachable. Raised by `accept`, `connect`."}, {"ENFILE", "number", " @type integer Too many open files in system.\n Raised by `accept`, `execve`, `mmap`, `open`, `pipe`, `socket`,\n `socketpair`, `swapon`, `timerfd_create`, `uselib`,\n `userfaultfd`."}, {"ENOBUFS", "number", " @type integer No buffer space available;\n Raised by `getpeername`, `getsockname`, `send`."}, {"ENODATA", "number", " @type integer No message is available in xsi stream or named pipe is being closed;\n no data available; barely in posix; returned by ioctl; very close in\n spirit to EPIPE?"}, {"ENODEV", "number", " @type integer No such device.\n Raised by `arch_prctl`, `mmap`, `open`, `prctl`, `timerfd_create`."}, {"ENOENT", "number", " @type integer No such file or directory.\n Raised by `access`, `bind`, `chdir`, `chmod`, `chown`, `chroot`,\n `clock_getres`, `execve`, `opendir`, `link`, `mkdir`, `mknod`,\n `open`, `readlink`, `rename`, `rmdir`, `stat`, `swapon`,\n `symlink`, `truncate`, `unlink`, `utime`, `utimensat`."}, {"ENOEXEC", "number", " @type integer Exec format error. Raised by `execve`, `uselib`."}, {"ENOLCK", "number", " @type integer No locks available. Raised by `fcntl`, `flock`."}, {"ENOLINK", "number", " @type integer Link has been severed."}, {"ENOMEDIUM", "number", " @type integer No medium found. Raised by `mount`."}, {"ENOMEM", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ENOMSG", "number", " @type integer Raised by `msgop`."}, {"ENONET", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ENOPROTOOPT", "number", " @type integer Protocol not available. Raised by `getsockopt`, `accept`."}, {"ENOSPC", "number", " @type integer No space left on device.\n Raised by `copy_file_range`, `fsync`, `link`, `mkdir`, `mknod`,\n `open`, `rename`, `symlink`, `sync_file_range`, `write`."}, {"ENOSR", "number", " @type integer Out of streams resources."}, {"ENOSTR", "number", " @type integer Device not a stream."}, {"ENOSYS", "number", " @type integer System call not available on this platform. On\n     Windows this is raised by `chroot`, `setuid`, `setgid`,\n     `getsid`, `setsid`, and others we're doing our best to\n     document."}, {"ENOTBLK", "number", " @type integer Block device required. Raised by `umount`."}, {"ENOTCONN", "number", " @type integer Socket is not connected.\n Raised by `getpeername`, `recv`, `send`, `shutdown`."}, {"ENOTDIR", "number", " @type integer Not a directory. This means that a directory\n     component in a supplied path *existed* but wasn't a\n     directory. For example, if you try to `open(\"foo/bar\")` and\n     `foo` is a regular file, then `ENOTDIR` will be returned.\n Raised by `open`, `access`, `chdir`, `chroot`, `execve`, `link`,\n `mkdir`, `mknod`, `opendir`, `readlink`, `rename`, `rmdir`,\n `stat`, `symlink`, `truncate`, `unlink`, `utimensat`, `bind`,\n `chmod`, `chown`, `fcntl`, `futimesat`."}, {"ENOTEMPTY", "number", " @type integer Directory not empty. Raised by `rmdir`."}, {"ENOTRECOVERABLE", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ENOTSOCK", "number", " @type integer Not a socket.\n Raised by `accept`, `bind`, `connect`, `getpeername`,\n `getsockname`, `getsockopt`, `listen`, `recv`, `send`,\n `shutdown`."}, {"ENOTSUP", "number", " @type integer Operation not supported.\n Raised by `chmod`, `clock_getres`, `clock_nanosleep`,\n `timer_create`."}, {"ENOTTY", "number", " @type integer Inappropriate i/o control operation. Raised by `ioctl`."}, {"ENXIO", "number", " @type integer No such device or address. Raised by `lseek`, `open`, `prctl`."}, {"EOPNOTSUPP", "number", " @type integer Socket operation not supported.\n Raised by accept, listen, mmap, prctl, readv, send,\n socketpair."}, {"EOVERFLOW", "number", " @type integer Raised by `copy_file_range`, `fanotify_init`, `lseek`, `mmap`,\n `open`, `stat`, `statfs`"}, {"EOWNERDEAD", "number", " @type integer"}, {"EPERM", "number", " @type integer Operation not permitted.\n Raised by `accept`, `chmod`, `chown`, `chroot`,\n `copy_file_range`, `execve`, `fallocate`, `fanotify_init`,\n `fcntl`, `futex`, `get_robust_list`, `getdomainname`,\n `getgroups`, `gethostname`, `getpriority`, `getrlimit`,\n `getsid`, `gettimeofday`, `idle`, `init_module`, `io_submit`,\n `ioctl_console`, `ioctl_ficlonerange`, `ioctl_fideduperange`,\n `ioperm`, `iopl`, `ioprio_set`, `keyctl`, `kill`, `link`,\n `lookup_dcookie`, `madvise`, `mbind`, `membarrier`,\n `migrate_pages`, `mkdir`, `mknod`, `mlock`, `mmap`, `mount`,\n `move_pages`, `msgctl`, `nice`, `open`, `open_by_handle_at`,\n `pciconfig_read`, `perf_event_open`, `pidfd_getfd`,\n `pidfd_send_signal`, `pivot_root`, `prctl`, `process_vm_readv`,\n `ptrace`, `quotactl`, `reboot`, `rename`, `request_key`,\n `rmdir`, `rt_sigqueueinfo`, `sched_setaffinity`,\n `sched_setattr`, `sched_setparam`, `sched_setscheduler`,\n `seteuid`, `setfsgid`, `setfsuid`, `setgid`, `setns`, `setpgid`,\n `setresuid`, `setreuid`, `setsid`, `setuid`, `setup`,\n `setxattr`, `sigaltstack`, `spu_create`, `stime`, `swapon`,\n `symlink`, `syslog`, `truncate`, `unlink`, `utime`, `utimensat`,\n `write`."}, {"EPFNOSUPPORT", "number", " @type integer Protocol family not supported."}, {"EPIPE", "number", " @type integer Broken pipe.\n Returned by `write`, `send`. This happens when you try\n to write data to a subprocess via a pipe but the reader end has\n already closed, possibly because the process died. Normally i/o\n routines only return this if `SIGPIPE` doesn't kill the process.\n Unlike default UNIX programs, redbean currently ignores `SIGPIPE` by\n default, so this error code is a distinct possibility when pipes or\n sockets are being used."}, {"EPROTO", "number", " @type integer Raised by `accept`, `connect`, `socket`, `socketpair`."}, {"EPROTONOSUPPORT", "number", " @type integer Protocol not supported. Raised by `socket`, `socketpair`."}, {"EPROTOTYPE", "number", " @type integer Protocol wrong type for socket. Raised by `connect`."}, {"ERANGE", "number", " @type integer Result too large.\n Raised by `prctl`."}, {"EREMOTE", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ERESTART", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ERFKILL", "number", " @type integer Operation not possible due to RF-kill."}, {"EROFS", "number", " @type integer Read-only filesystem.\n Raised by access, bind, chmod, chown, link, mkdir, mknod, open,\n rename, rmdir, symlink, truncate, unlink, utime, utimensat."}, {"ESHUTDOWN", "number", " @type integer Cannot send after transport endpoint shutdown; note that shutdown write is an `EPIPE`."}, {"ESOCKTNOSUPPORT", "number", " @type integer Socket type not supported."}, {"ESPIPE", "number", " @type integer Invalid seek.\n Raised by `lseek`, `splice`, `sync_file_range`."}, {"ESRCH", "number", " @type integer No such process.\n Raised by `getpriority`, `getrlimit`, `getsid`, `ioprio_set`, `kill`, `setpgid`, `tkill`, `utimensat`."}, {"ESTALE", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ETIME", "number", " @type integer Timer expired. Raised by `connect`."}, {"ETIMEDOUT", "number", " @type integer Connection timed out. Raised by `connect`."}, {"ETOOMANYREFS", "number", " @type integer Too many references: cannot splice. Raised by `sendmsg`."}, {"ETXTBSY", "number", " @type integer Won't open executable that's executing in write mode.\n Raised by access, copy_file_range, execve, mmap, open, truncate."}, {"EUSERS", "number", " @type integer"}, {"EXDEV", "number", " @type integer Improper link.\n Raised by copy_file_range, link, rename."}, {"FD_CLOEXEC", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_GETFD", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_GETFL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_GETLK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_OK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_RDLCK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_SETFD", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_SETFL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_SETLK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_SETLKW", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_UNLCK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"F_WRLCK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IPPROTO_ICMP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IPPROTO_IP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IPPROTO_RAW", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IPPROTO_TCP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IPPROTO_UDP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_DROP_MEMBERSHIP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_HDRINCL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_MTU", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_MULTICAST_IF", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_MULTICAST_LOOP", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_MULTICAST_TTL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_OPTIONS", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_PKTINFO", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_RECVTOS", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_RECVTTL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_TOS", "number", " @type integer"}, {"IP_TTL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ITIMER_PROF", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ITIMER_REAL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"ITIMER_VIRTUAL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_ALERT", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_CRIT", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_DEBUG", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_EMERG", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_ERR", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_INFO", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_NOTICE", "number", " @type integer"}, {"LOG_WARNING", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_CTRUNC", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_DONTROUTE", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_DONTWAIT", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_NOSIGNAL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_OOB", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_PEEK", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_TRUNC", "number", " @type integer"}, {"MSG_WAITALL", "number", " @type integer"}, {"NAME_MAX", "number", " @type integer  Returns maximum length of file path component.\n POSIX requires this be at least 14. Most operating systems define it\n as 255. It's a good idea to not exceed 253 since that's the limit on\n DNS labels."}, {"NSIG", "number", " @type integer Returns maximum number of signals supported by underlying system.\n The limit for unix.Sigset is 128 to support FreeBSD, but most\n operating systems define this much lower, like 32. This constant\n reflects the value chosen by the underlying operating system."}, {"O_RDONLY", "number", " @type integer open for reading (default)"}, {"O_WRONLY", "number", " @type integer open for writing"}, {"O_RDWR", "number", " @type integer open for reading and writing"}, {"O_CREAT", "number", " @type integer create file if it doesn't exist"}, {"O_TRUNC", "number", " @type integer automatic `ftruncate(fd, 0)` if exists"}, {"O_CLOEXEC", "number", " @type integer automatic `close()` upon `execve()`"}, {"O_EXCL", "number", " @type integer exclusive access (see below)"}, {"O_APPEND", "number", " @type integer open file for append only"}, {"O_NONBLOCK", "number", " @type integer asks read/write to fail with EAGAIN rather than block"}, {"O_DIRECT", "number", " @type integer it's complicated (not supported on Apple and OpenBSD)"}, {"O_DIRECTORY", "number", " @type integer useful for stat'ing (hint on UNIX but required on NT)"}, {"O_NOFOLLOW", "number", " @type integer fail if it's a symlink (zero on Windows)"}, {"O_UNLINK", "number", " @type integer automatically delete file upon close()"}, {"O_PATH", "number", " @type integer open a path reference only, without read/write access\n (Linux only; fails with EINVAL elsewhere). Usable with landlock\n rule paths and *at() calls even when the path itself is unreadable."}, {"O_DSYNC", "number", " @type integer it's complicated (zero on non-Linux/Apple)"}, {"O_SYNC", "number", " @type integer synchronize i/o operations appropriately"}, {"O_NOATIME", "number", " @type integer don't record access time (zero on non-Linux)"}, {"O_ACCMODE", "number", " @type integer"}, {"O_NOCTTY", "number", " @type integer"}, {"PATH_MAX", "number", " @type integer Returns maximum length of file path.\n This applies to a complete path being passed to system calls.\n POSIX.1 XSI requires this be at least 1024 so that's what most\n platforms support. On Windows NT, the limit is technically 260\n characters. Your redbean works around that by prefixing `//?/`\n to your paths as needed. On Linux this limit will be 4096, but\n that won't be the case for functions such as realpath that are\n implemented at the C library level; however such functions are\n the exception rather than the norm, and report `enametoolong()`,\n when exceeding the libc limit."}, {"PLEDGE_PENALTY_KILL_THREAD", "number", " the default on Linux. It's effectively the same as killing the\n process, since redbean has no threads. The termination signal\n can't be caught and will be either `SIGSYS` or `SIGABRT`.\n Consider enabling stderr logging below so you'll know why your\n program failed. Otherwise check the system log."}, {"PLEDGE_PENALTY_KILL_PROCESS", "number", " This is always the case on OpenBSD."}, {"PLEDGE_PENALTY_RETURN_EPERM", "number", " instead of killing. This is a gentler solution that allows code to\n display a friendly warning. Please note this may lead to weird\n behaviors if the software being sandboxed is lazy about checking\n error results."}, {"PLEDGE_STDERR_LOGGING", "number", " know which promises are needed whenever violations occur. Without\n this, violations will be logged to `dmesg` on Linux if the penalty\n is to kill the process. You would then need to manually look up\n the system call number and then cross reference it with the\n cosmopolitan libc `pledge()` documentation. You can also use\n `strace -ff` which is easier. This is ignored OpenBSD, which\n already has a good system log. Turning on stderr logging (which\n uses SECCOMP trapping) also means that the `unix.WTERMSIG()` on\n your killed processes will always be `unix.SIGABRT` on both Linux\n and OpenBSD. Otherwise, Linux prefers to raise `unix.SIGSYS`."}, {"PIPE_BUF", "number", " @type integer Returns maximum size at which pipe i/o is guaranteed atomic.\n POSIX requires this be at least 512. Linux is more generous and\n allows 4096. On Windows NT this is currently 4096, and it's the\n parameter redbean passes to `CreateNamedPipe()`."}, {"POLLERR", "number", " @t